# SFT execution status v0.111

2026-09-24. The write-guard policy and scalar reservation core are implemented
and locally tested. **This is not native write-limit enforcement; Gate 5 remains
incomplete.** No kernel filter/listener, remote action, payload read, GPU work,
inference or training occurred. The seven new files remain local and unpushed.

## Completed

- Two fixed cBPF candidates: a temporary bootstrap listener notifies every
  `write`/`pwrite64`, and a closed final seal rejects other data-write channels
  and further policy installation. All descriptors are counted. Neither filter
  alone provides the intended meter, and the temporary listener is unsafe alone.
- The broker preserves full unsigned requested-byte counts and reserves before
  either ID-validation or response callback. Zero-length writes consume the
  notification limit. Stale IDs, duplicates, budget exhaustion, callback
  ambiguity/cancellation, reentry, ownership and deadline failures are terminal;
  no refund, retry or budget reset is offered.
- Exact candidate wire parsing discards PID/IP/FD/buffer/offset fields. Only
  aggregate snapshots and fixed error codes are retention-safe. Exception
  objects and tracebacks can retain private frames and must not be stored.

The public installer and server always return `host_guard_not_bound`. Component
limits are bounded planning inputs, not approved native-run budgets. The actual
SFT resource envelope and historical host contract are unchanged. Requested
data-byte accounting is not physical disk/journal or metadata accounting;
creation/mode/fsync effects and sparse-file growth still need separate bounds.

## Verification

**608 unique tests pass on CPython 3.11.15 and 3.13.7**, with no failures, errors
or skips: 37 policy tests, 50 broker tests and 521 earlier regressions.

An independent cBPF interpreter verifies dispatch, x32/foreign-architecture
rejection, full-width flags, all-FD write notification, alternate-channel denial
and two-filter precedence. The integration case passes twelve generated
notifications through both policy decisions into the real broker core, checking
precharge and exact response bytes without invoking the kernel.

Independent review found no accounting or cBPF control-flow bypass in this local
model. A fixed ENOSYS refusal for `clone3` keeps that syscall denied while
permitting a potential libc fallback to the flag-checked legacy thread syscall;
actual native compatibility is not established by these tests.

Syntax/AST, 100-column and whitespace checks pass. Ruff remains unavailable at
the previous temporary path; no replacement was downloaded. All 53 previous
readiness/development bindings, 78 qualified native source rows and 39 worker
source rows match. Earlier evidence/manifests are unchanged, and tracked/staged
diffs are empty.

